{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"599f2bc6a3ba7131","name":"card_event_compromise","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/France/events/card_event_compromise","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"COMPROMISE","text":"Choose One - Draw a card and gain +3 HQ defense OR do this twice for both players.","flavorText":"Free France’s military input in the total Allied war effort was fairly modest in comparison with Britain and the USA, and de Gaulle’s influence on his allies' decisions was limited. From 1940 to 1944, he insisted on having the Free French troops involved in many operations alongside the larger Allied forces, in the North African and the Italian campaigns. Political and military cooperation on the ground were achieved, because it was agreed France needed to earn its place amongst the victors, even if only up to its small means at the time. But de Gaulle took offense when Churchill summoned him to London, on June 4th 1944, to inform him about D-Day. He was also told no French troops were to be involved. In the meantime, Allied preparation to liberate Europe from German occupation also included planning for a new currency to be used for military purchasing in combat zones, and for the restoration of monetary order in liberated regions. The Allies landed in Normandy carrying “Allied Military francs”, printed in the USA, and authorized by General Eisenhower, but de Gaulle denounced these as “counterfeit currency”. This illustrates how, even amongst Western Allies, partnership wasn’t always an “Entente Cordiale”, given the huge stakes at play following liberation, over the political and economical control of liberated areas. Each side pushed strongly for their own interests, and ultimately they had to negotiate and to compromise. France wasn’t really involved in D-Day (only 209 French infantry soldiers landed that day), but managed to safeguard its monetary sovereignty.","faction":"France","type":"order","rarity":"Common","cost":4,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_compromise","title":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_event_compromise_title","SourceString":"COMPROMISE","LocalizedString":"COMPROMISE"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::France","kredits":4,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_event_compromise_text","SourceString":"Choose One - Draw a card and gain +3 HQ defense OR do this twice for both players.","LocalizedString":"Choose One - Draw a card and gain +3 HQ defense OR do this twice for both players."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_event_compromise_history","SourceString":"Free France’s military input in the total Allied war effort was fairly modest in comparison with Britain and the USA, and de Gaulle’s influence on his allies' decisions was limited. From 1940 to 1944, he insisted on having the Free French troops involved in many operations alongside the larger Allied forces, in the North African and the Italian campaigns. Political and military cooperation on the ground were achieved, because it was agreed France needed to earn its place amongst the victors, even if only up to its small means at the time. But de Gaulle took offense when Churchill summoned him to London, on June 4th 1944, to inform him about D-Day. He was also told no French troops were to be involved. In the meantime, Allied preparation to liberate Europe from German occupation also included planning for a new currency to be used for military purchasing in combat zones, and for the restoration of monetary order in liberated regions. The Allies landed in Normandy carrying “Allied Military francs”, printed in the USA, and authorized by General Eisenhower, but de Gaulle denounced these as “counterfeit currency”. This illustrates how, even amongst Western Allies, partnership wasn’t always an “Entente Cordiale”, given the huge stakes at play following liberation, over the political and economical control of liberated areas. Each side pushed strongly for their own interests, and ultimately they had to negotiate and to compromise. France wasn’t really involved in D-Day (only 209 French infantry soldiers landed that day), but managed to safeguard its monetary sovereignty.","LocalizedString":"Free France’s military input in the total Allied war effort was fairly modest in comparison with Britain and the USA, and de Gaulle’s influence on his allies' decisions was limited. From 1940 to 1944, he insisted on having the Free French troops involved in many operations alongside the larger Allied forces, in the North African and the Italian campaigns. Political and military cooperation on the ground were achieved, because it was agreed France needed to earn its place amongst the victors, even if only up to its small means at the time. But de Gaulle took offense when Churchill summoned him to London, on June 4th 1944, to inform him about D-Day. He was also told no French troops were to be involved. In the meantime, Allied preparation to liberate Europe from German occupation also included planning for a new currency to be used for military purchasing in combat zones, and for the restoration of monetary order in liberated regions. The Allies landed in Normandy carrying “Allied Military francs”, printed in the USA, and authorized by General Eisenhower, but de Gaulle denounced these as “counterfeit currency”. This illustrates how, even amongst Western Allies, partnership wasn’t always an “Entente Cordiale”, given the huge stakes at play following liberation, over the political and economical control of liberated areas. Each side pushed strongly for their own interests, and ultimately they had to negotiate and to compromise. France wasn’t really involved in D-Day (only 209 French infantry soldiers landed that day), but managed to safeguard its monetary sovereignty."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Common","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::CurrentExpansion","isReserved":true,"chooseOneCards":[{"cardText":{"Namespac